From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 15:36:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] iov_iter: tell the host of the page a bvec copy-out wrote

A copy into an ITER_BVEC destination (a loop device's read of its
backing file, an io_uring fixed-buffer read of a buffered file, nbd,
nfsd, sunrpc) writes each page through the kernel's alias, and the
page may be one a process runs: on x86-64 the host translates the
code of such a page and has to hear of the write, which the flush at
the pin, before the copy, did not tell it. End each destination step
with flush_dcache_page(), as memcpy_to_page() and memzero_page() do.
The call is empty where the architecture has no hook. The x86-64
hook costs a barrier and a bit test for a page no process runs,
names the frame of one a process does, and warns once and drops the
announcement in a context that is not a task, as it always has.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 include/linux/iov_iter.h | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/include/linux/iov_iter.h b/include/linux/iov_iter.h
index f9a17fbbd3980b1fec5f9a7fde812aebef6728d5..20568cbbec5eceb2cd85245ad03c58d1778fd477 100644
--- a/include/linux/iov_iter.h
+++ b/include/linux/iov_iter.h
@@ -123,6 +123,14 @@ size_t iterate_bvec(struct iov_iter *iter, size_t len, void *priv, void *priv2,
 		remain = step(kaddr + offset % PAGE_SIZE, progress, part, priv, priv2);
 		kunmap_local(kaddr);
 		consumed = part - remain;
+		/*
+		 * A copy into a destination page writes it through the kernel's
+		 * alias, as a driver that fills pages itself does, and the page
+		 * may be one a process runs: end the write with
+		 * flush_dcache_page() where the architecture has a hook for it.
+		 */
+		if (consumed && !iter->data_source)
+			flush_dcache_page(p->bv_page + offset / PAGE_SIZE);
 		len -= consumed;
 		progress += consumed;
 		skip += consumed;
